WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.720
In Winnie the Pooh: Blood and Honey,&nbsp;
a bloodthirsty Pooh hunts for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.360
unsuspecting victims. Here's a&nbsp;
look at the beloved character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.540
now transformed from a honey-loving&nbsp;
bear into a walking nightmare.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:13.380
Ever since the Winnie the Pooh: Blood and&nbsp;
Honey trailer hit the Internet back in August,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:17.040
it has become clear that this is not&nbsp;
your average Pooh flick. Very loosely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:21.780
adapted from the work of A.A. Milne and E.H.&nbsp;
Shepard, the film purports to introduce Pooh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:26.940
as a bloodthirsty beast hellbent on eliminating&nbsp;
all humans from his path. As for Piglet, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.900
he doesn't get off too easy, either. In this&nbsp;
version of the story, the adorable pig is now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.740
a total psycho swine who will further&nbsp;
Pooh's murderous mission at all costs.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:39.840
Just like his character, Pooh himself has been&nbsp;
distorted beyond recognition. Grotesque and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.100
barbaric, he strikes the imposing figure of a&nbsp;
beast, all while wielding a terrifying plastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.780
grin. And while Pooh's appearance makes sense in&nbsp;
the context of the film — Pooh is essentially a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:53.640
mindless animal now, clobbered by life's cruelties&nbsp;
— his soulless face still sends chills down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:58.260
any Pooh fan's spine. But the best part is that&nbsp;
the actor who plays him looks completely innocent.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.320
According to Blood and Honey's&nbsp;
director, Rhys Frake-Waterfield,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.080
the new Pooh is nothing like the&nbsp;
friendly bear that children have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.560
loved since the early 20th century.&nbsp;
As he explained to Variety last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.400
Pooh and Piglet were left alone long ago by&nbsp;
Christopher Robin in the world of this film.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:16.560
"Pooh, Piglett, Eyeore. We were friends&nbsp;
for many years. And they’re out there."

00:01:16.944 --> 00:01:18.204
He told Variety,

00:01:18.204 --> 00:01:19.920
"They've essentially become feral. They're like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.980
a vicious bear and pig who want to&nbsp;
go around and try and find prey."

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:27.240
Naturally, a formidable-looking actor would&nbsp;
have to take on such a terrifying role. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:31.620
Craig David Dowsett is, by all accounts, an&nbsp;
average guy. He's still an up-and-coming actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:35.160
so he hasn't done many interviews yet, but&nbsp;
he's perfectly charming in those that have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:39.120
made the rounds so far. On top of that, he's&nbsp;
already set to appear in another horror film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.880
so Blood and Honey fans can expect&nbsp;
to see more of him in the future.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:45.720
Before now, Craig David Dowsett's only&nbsp;
acting credit was for the horror flick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:50.400
The Area 51 Incident, which was also directed&nbsp;
by Frake-Waterfield. But after Blood and Honey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.840
Dowsett will appear in yet another horror film,&nbsp;
The House that Zombies Built. Not much is known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:59.100
about the film yet, but Dowsett is currently&nbsp;
credited as "Roger the Zombie Easter Bunny," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:02.280
could signal another childhood-destroying&nbsp;
role as a beloved fantasy figure.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.720
But perhaps most auspicious for future fans of&nbsp;
Blood and Honey is the news that Frake-Waterfield&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.640
plans to make more childhood-ruining cinematic&nbsp;
adaptations of beloved fantasy figures. As he told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.720
The Hollywood Reporter, he's already cooked up a&nbsp;
story involving an evil Peter Pan and overweight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:20.220
Tinkerbell, who is recovering from drug addiction,&nbsp;
in Peter Pan's Neverland Nightmare. Additionally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:24.180
Frake-Waterfield plans to make Bambi: The&nbsp;
Reckoning, which will tell the classic story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:28.320
of a rabid baby deer who seeks violent revenge&nbsp;
upon the humans who sought to destroy his life.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.760
Of course, since Dowsett and Frake-Waterfield&nbsp;
have collaborated twice now, it tracks that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.300
they might continue their working relationship&nbsp;
on Frake-Waterfield's future horror outings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:40.320
After all, Dowsett looks appropriately&nbsp;
horrifying in his low-budget Pooh get-up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:44.700
so it's not hard to imagine him filling in the&nbsp;
shoes of a larger-than-life, fanged Bambi — or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:49.320
of a lumbering, deadly Peter Pan, for that matter.&nbsp;
Now, we can only hope that Blood and Honey drums&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.980
up enough hype to fund better costumes for&nbsp;
those hypothetical horror flicks of the future.

00:02:54.946 --> 00:02:57.482
*Screams* 
 
 

